---
import ArticleShell from '../../components/ArticleShell.astro';
import { claim, articleFacts } from '../../lib/articles.js';
import { withArticle } from '../../lib/grammar.js';
import { STAGES } from '../../lib/hue.js';

/**
 * Artikel: de leeftijd waarop jongeren het huis uit gaan. Het nieuws in de data: het
 * Europese cijfer staat stil sinds de reeksbreuk, maar de landen kruipen naar elkaar toe.
 * Alle getallen uit src/lib/articles.js; schrijfregels in research/writing-rules.md.
 */
const { h, t, g, source, fmt } = articleFacts('leaving-home');
const W = (name: string) => withArticle(name);
const first = t.moved[0];
const last = t.moved.at(-1);
const exceptions = g.exceptions.map((c: any) => c.name);

// Zinnen die niet uit één getal volgen; zie claim() in src/lib/articles.js.
claim(Math.abs(h.eu.total - t.euThen) < 0.5, "Europe's leaving-home age is flat");
claim(t.spreadNow < t.spreadThen, 'The gap between countries is closing');
claim(g.exceptions.length <= g.of / 10, 'Young women leave first, almost everywhere');

const title = `Europe's leaving-home age is flat. Its countries are not`;
const description =
  `Half of young people in the EU had left home by ${fmt(h.eu.total)} in ${h.year}, almost the same as in ${t.since}. ` +
  `Behind that flat figure, the gap between countries shrank from ${fmt(t.spreadThen)} to ${fmt(t.spreadNow)} years.`;
---

<ArticleShell
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/notes/leaving-home-europe-converging/"
  hue={STAGES.leave}
  published="2026-11-21"
  metricPath="/leaving-home/"
  metricLabel="Leaving home"
  cta="See the age for every country, and where your own age falls"
  source={source}
>
  <p slot="lead">
    Half of young people in the European Union had left their parents' home by the age of
    {' '}<b>{fmt(h.eu.total)}</b> in {h.year}, according to Eurostat. That is almost exactly where it
    stood in {t.since} ({fmt(t.euThen)}). The countries behind that average have moved a lot more.
  </p>

  <h2>The gap between countries is closing</h2>
  <p>
    In {t.since} the earliest and the latest country were {fmt(t.spreadThen)} years apart. In {t.until}
    it was {fmt(t.spreadNow)} years. Over those years the age fell by at least half a year in
    {' '}{t.earlier.length} of the {t.countries} countries and rose by that much in {t.later.length}. In the
    other {t.stable} it barely moved.
  </p>
  <p>
    The biggest fall was in {W(first.name)}, from {fmt(first.series[t.since])} to {fmt(first.total)}.
    The biggest rise was in {W(last.name)}, from {fmt(last.series[t.since])} to {fmt(last.total)}. A
    European figure that stands still can hide exactly this: countries that leave late leaving a
    little earlier, and countries that leave early leaving a little later.
  </p>

  <h2>Still {fmt(h.gap)} years between the earliest and the latest country</h2>
  <p>
    The distance is still large. In {W(h.earliest.name)} half of young people had left home by
    {' '}{fmt(h.earliest.total)} in {h.year}. In {W(h.latest.name)} that point came at {fmt(h.latest.total)}:
    {' '}{fmt(h.gap)} years later, for the same step in life.
  </p>

  <h2>Young women leave first, almost everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    In {g.womenLower} of the {g.of} countries young women reach that point earlier than young men, by
    {' '}{fmt(g.mean)} years on average. The widest gap is in {W(g.widest.name)}, at {fmt(g.widestGap)}
    {' '}years. {exceptions.length === 1 ? <>The only exception is {W(exceptions[0])}.</> : exceptions.length > 1 ? <>The exceptions are {exceptions.map(W).join(', ')}.</> : null}
  </p>

  <h2>What these figures do not say</h2>
  <p>
    Eurostat calls this figure an average age, but its own definition describes a midpoint: the age
    by which half of young people no longer live with their parents. That is not the same thing, and
    it matters most where the spread is lopsided.
  </p>
  <p>
    The figures also do not say why people leave when they do. That would need data on housing,
    study and work that is not in this dataset, so this note does not guess.
  </p>
  <div class="limits">
    <p>
      The comparison starts in {t.since}, not earlier. Eurostat rebuilt the survey behind these figures
      in 2021, and almost every country carries a break there. Comparing across it would show changes
      that happened to the survey, not to young people. <a href="/notes/eurostat-2021-series-break/">Why that break matters more than a footnote.</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</ArticleShell>
